{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793729931,"nonce":"U8d1tRs7lTTRQuuoNdStlnpBmNU=","title":"Forward R2 editor assets without copying chunks through Worker WASM","message":"Parent: 52138fcda83502ec87679713955f7cf7c7f7e8654a0ef55ba60357bd0f22a904\n\nStaging startup fails in thread.js while instantiateStreaming loads ash_browser_bg.wasm. Chrome reports a section extending past the end at offset 658191, with different remaining lengths for concurrent agents. Its Network panel shows several decoded responses ending around 721-782 KB instead of 968,773 bytes. Downloading that R2 object through the authenticated API verifies the exact local SHA-256 and compiles successfully. This distinguishes delivery truncation from a corrupt source or uploaded object.\n\nThe editor asset handler used ObjectBody::stream() followed by Response::from_stream(), copying every R2 chunk through the Rust/WASM request. worker 0.8.7 explicitly documents ObjectBody::response_body() as direct runtime streaming that avoids Worker CPU consumption while forwarding. Replace that copying path, preserving response metadata, isolation headers, ETags and authorization. Resource termination is a likely cause of the variable truncation; no live exceededCpu outcome has yet been captured.\n\nChecks: build the Rust Worker; run existing large-asset, conditional-response and isolation checks; concurrently fetch and compile the real WASM host through both app and asset routes; deploy to staging and reload the failing browser page. The eight focused asset/prebuilt checks pass locally. Complete only after deployed browser startup is confirmed.","files":null}]}